From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: android-kitkat-tls12 <android-kitkat-tls12@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/32] jsse.enableSNIExtension other than true/false keeps SNI
 on

The SNI-by-default port (libcore/0013, conscrypt 966ae8a) threw a RuntimeException
from every client handshake when jsse.enableSNIExtension held any value
other than "true" or "false". KitKat never read the property, so an app
that set it to some other value would lose every TLS connection after
the upgrade, and the unchecked exception escapes callers that catch
only IOException (okhttp, many app HTTP stacks).

Now "true" and "false" (any case) work as before, and any other value
keeps SNI on, the default, with one warning per process
(System.logW).

Tests (SSLSocketTest, on the ClientHello capture of 0012):
- test_SSLSocket_SNI_propertyIgnoresCase: TRUE/True/true send SNI,
  FALSE/False do not;
- test_SSLSocket_SNI_invalidPropertyKeepsSniOn: "yes", "0", "",
  " true" send SNI and the handshake starts (failed before: the client
  thread threw the RuntimeException);
- test_SSLSocket_SNI_setHostnameNotFqdn: setHostname("localhost") and
  setHostname("") send no SNI (isValidSniHostname wants a dot).

Android 4.2 port (android-4.2.2_r1) of patch 0027 of the 4.4 series
(renumbered: the 4.4 patch 0025, the ssl.SocketFactory.provider hook of
the TLS 1.3 layer, is not part of this series), made from its Android
4.3 port. Paths and names: libcore/crypto of 4.4 (org.conscrypt,
org_conscrypt_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacrypto) is libcore/luni on 4.2
(org.apache.harmony.xnet.provider.jsse,
org_apache_harmony_xnet_provider_jsse_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacore).
Otherwise the same change; only line offsets differ.
